{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Any devoted thing that is devoted of man shall not be redeemed; he shall surely be put to death.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: one who is going forth to execution and says, 'His valuation is upon me' — he has said nothing; 'he shall surely be put to death' — he is going to death, therefore 'he shall not be redeemed,' for he has no monetary value and no valuation." "<b>But</b> the Ramban, of blessed memory, explained that this verse, by way of the plain sense (peshat), says that whoever devotes a ḥerem of his own — whether man, beast, or field of his possession — it is holy to HaShem, for these are ḥermei kohanim (devoted gifts to the priests) that have no redemption. But one who devotes a ḥerem of something that is not his, such as those who wage war against their enemies and vow a vow: 'If You will indeed deliver this people into my hand, I will devote their cities' — in this case the intention of the one vowing is certainly not to give them to the priests, but that everything be forbidden for benefit, and they shall put to death all who are found in them, for his intention is to destroy and annihilate them. And from this verse it can be proven that any king of Israel or a Great Sanhedrin in the assembly of all Israel, who have authority in matters of law — if they decree a ḥerem on a matter, or if they decree a ḥerem on a city when they wage war upon it, the one who transgresses this is liable to death. And this is the liability of the men of Jabesh-gilead (Judges 21:9), who transgressed the oath of the congregation and did not come to Mizpah; and this is also the liability of Jonathan, for his father said to him (I Samuel 14:44): 'כה יעשה אלהים וכה יוסיף כי מות תמות יונתן.' And regarding what is written about Jonathan (there): 'ויפדו העם את יונתן ולא מת' — 'And the people redeemed Jonathan and he did not die' — when he was worthy of death because he transgressed the oath of his father the king, the reason is that he was unaware (shogeg), and this is what Jonathan translated (there) in 'כי עם אלהים עשה היום הזה' — 'ארי קדם ה׳ גלי דבשלו עבד יומא דין.'" "<b>And from this</b> go forth and learn the error of Jephthah, who erred here in offering his daughter as a burnt offering. For he thought that just as a king of Israel who vows or devotes something — his ḥerem or his vow takes effect to put a man or men to death, and likewise anyone who transgresses his ḥerem is liable to death — so too when he vows in time of war to make a sacrifice of a man or men, the vow should take effect. And he certainly erred greatly in this, for when the ḥerem of a king and the Sanhedrin and their oath takes effect, it is only upon those who rebel against them or upon one who transgresses their decree and ordinances; but it does not take effect to make a sacrifice of something that is not fit for a sacrifice. And this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'Even consecration of its monetary value is required' — and the act he performed proved that he was not a scholar of Torah." "<b>And do not</b> be persuaded by the words of R. Abraham, who says that the meaning of 'והעליתיהו עולה לה׳' is to say: if the one coming out of the doors of my house, whether man or woman, shall be 'לה׳' — that is, he shall be separated entirely from the ways of the world to serve in the name of HaShem in prayer and thanksgiving to HaShem; and if the matter is fit to be offered, I will offer it as a burnt offering; and Jephthah built a house for his daughter outside the city, and she secluded herself there and was sustained all her days, and no man knew her, and she remained bound there. And these are words of spirit (delusion), for a vow that is 'לה׳' does not mean to be a Nazirite-like ascetic, but rather as Samuel, whose mother said (I Samuel 1:11): 'ונתתיו לה׳' — 'and I will give him to HaShem' — and he would serve in the house of HaShem, not as an ascetic. And according to the laws of the Torah, it is not in a person's power to vow concerning the one who goes out from the door of his house that they be ascetics, just as it is not in his power to offer them as a burnt offering. And if the matter were so, then his daughter who wept over her virginity, and her companions with her, would be like prostitutes soliciting their hire, for they would set four days a year to console Jephthah's daughter because she did not marry a husband — and far be it that such should be a statute in Israel, for it is still possible for her to serve HaShem in purity. Rather, the matter was literally as the plain sense of the verse: he offered his daughter as a burnt offering; thus wrote the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>And in the Midrash</b> (Proverbs 11:30): 'פרי צדיק עץ חיים ולוקח נפשות חכם' — 'The fruit of the righteous is a tree of life, and he who takes souls is wise.' If a person is righteous but does not engage in Torah, he is nothing; rather, 'פרי צדיק עץ חיים' — as it says (Proverbs 3): 'עץ חיים היא למחזיקים בה' — for through being a scholar of Torah he will learn how to 'take souls,' as it says: 'ולוקח נפשות חכם.' You find in Jephthah that because he was not a scholar of Torah, he lost his daughter. When? At the time he waged war against the children of Ammon and vowed, as it says (Judges 11:30–31): 'וידר יפתח נדר לה׳ ויאמר אם נתון תתן את בני עמון בידי והיה היוצא אשר יצא מדלתי ביתי לקראתי והעליתיהו עולה לה׳,' and it is written (there): 'והנה בתו יוצאת לקראתו בתופים ובמחולות,' and it is written: 'ויהי כראותו אותה ויקרע את בגדיו ויאמר אהה בתי הכרע הכרעתיני ואת היית בעוכרי.' Now Phinehas was there, but because of the arrogance that was in Jephthah — that he did not wish to go before him — his daughter was lost. Jephthah said: 'I am the judge of Israel, the head of the commanders; shall I humble myself and go before him?' Phinehas said: 'I am the High Priest, the son of High Priests; shall I go to an ignoramus?' Between the two of them, this poor one was lost from the world. And both of them were punished on her account: from Phinehas the holy spirit departed; Jephthah's limbs were scattered at his death, as it says (Judges 12): 'ויקבר בערי גלעד.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "The Targum of Jonathan's words in Judges 21:9 is actually at I Samuel 14:45; the citation appears to reference the Targum on that passage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:27.780986270Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}